      "name": null,
      "problem": "The surface $S,$ in square centimeters, of a cylinder whose height equals the radius of its base varies directly with the **square** of its radius $r,$ measured in centimeters. If the surface is $16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ when the radius is $2\\,\\text{cm},$ then what is the constant of variation?",
      "worked_solution": "The surface $S$ is directly proportional to the radius $r,$ so we have\n\n$$\nS=k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $S=16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ when $r=2\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16\\pi  & =k\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 16\\pi  & =k\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{16\\pi }{4} & =k \\\\ 4\\pi  & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of variation is $k=4\\pi .$",
